 \subsection{Transmit Budget and Link Lease}
 
-The transmit queue holds four 1024-byte frames. Each background pass sends at most 16 bytes and only up to \texttt{availableForWrite()}. Periodic telemetry is admitted only while two queue slots remain available, leaving room for acknowledgments and events. A mutation does not run if its acknowledgment cannot be queued. The client must time out and resynchronize after congestion; it must not assume an unacknowledged command ran.
-
-On expiry the lease handler first requests OFF and enable inhibition, then disarms the lease, stops streaming, and marks an event pending. It does not save flash. Reconnection needs a new lease and explicit ON. This is a background-loop communication guard; it does not disconnect mains, discharge capacitors, or replace an independent hardware interlock.
+Four 1024-byte frames are held in the transmit queue. In each background pass, at most 16 bytes are sent and only up to \texttt{availableForWrite()}. Periodic telemetry is admitted only while two queue slots remain available, leaving room for acknowledgments and events. A mutation is not run if its acknowledgment cannot be queued. A client timeout and resynchronization are required after congestion; execution of an unacknowledged command must not be assumed.
+
+On expiry, OFF and enable inhibition are first requested by the lease handler, then the lease is disarmed, streaming is stopped, and an event is marked pending. Flash is not saved. For reconnection, a new lease and explicit ON are required. This is a background-loop communication guard; mains is not disconnected, capacitors are not discharged, and an independent hardware interlock is not replaced.
 
