 \section*{Abstract}
 \addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
 
-I built and tested a USB-C laboratory power supply using an earlier DTU charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant converter and supplied planar magnetics. I integrated it into working hardware and developed the controller. The detachable load created the main control problem: when demand disappeared, energy remained in the resonant path and capacitors. I needed to regulate the loaded supply and limit further charging at no load.
+A USB-C laboratory power supply was built and tested using an earlier DTU charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant converter and supplied planar magnetics. The converter was integrated into working hardware, and the controller was developed for a detachable load. The main control problem was the energy remaining in the resonant path and capacitors when demand disappeared. Regulation under load and limited further charging at no load were required.
 
-I kept the carrier fixed at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz} and used the STM32G071 to adjust energy transfer through the GaN half bridge's burst-enable interval. I moved feedback acquisition to circular direct memory access (DMA) so analog reads would not block the control loop. The final PWR-1.9.20 controller regulates the isolated secondary bulk bus, uses limited precharge while feedback starts, and applies different duty limits under load and at no load. A downstream output stage provides the tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings.
+The carrier was kept fixed at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, and energy transfer was adjusted through the GaN half bridge's burst-enable interval using the STM32G071. Feedback acquisition was moved to circular direct memory access (DMA) so the control loop would not be blocked by analog reads. The isolated secondary bulk bus is regulated by the final PWR-1.9.20 controller, limited precharge is used while feedback starts, and different duty limits are applied under load and at no load. The tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings are provided by a downstream output stage.
 
-At a logged input of \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz}, the matched \SI{20}{\volt} measurements gave \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with recorded peak-to-peak ripple of \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt}. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. The input--output pairs give a screening efficiency of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent}; uncertain timing and calibration limit the accuracy of that estimate. The no-load and load-removal captures show the controller operating through the tested events. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a \SI{90.4}{\celsius} surface maximum. The next priorities are to measure losses, improve heat removal, and test protection timing.
+At a logged input of \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz}, matched \SI{20}{\volt} measurements were used to obtain output powers of \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with recorded peak-to-peak ripple of \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt}. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. A screening efficiency of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent} is calculated from the input--output pairs; uncertain timing and calibration limit the accuracy of that estimate. Controller operation through the tested no-load and load-removal events is documented in the captures. In a thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere}, a \SI{90.4}{\celsius} surface maximum was recorded. The next priorities are to measure losses, improve heat removal, and test protection timing.
 
-I also document the V2 STM32F410 implementation, PWR-2.0.1 firmware, and browser control and logging tools. The appendices include the full controller and supporting source. The revised July--October plan calls for a V2 retest, diagnosis of the power shortfall, and a V3 edge-card design. It also calls for a common method for comparing power density. Powered V2 characterization and the V3 comparison remain open.
+The V2 STM32F410 implementation, PWR-2.0.1 firmware, and browser control and logging tools are also documented. The full controller and supporting source are included in the appendices. A V2 retest, diagnosis of the power shortfall, and a V3 edge-card design are called for in the revised July--October plan. A common method for comparing power density is also called for. Powered V2 characterization and the V3 comparison remain open.
 
-I demonstrated digitally controlled operation at approximately \SI{80}{\watt}. The revised plan keeps a \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and a \SI{65}{\watt} continuous minimum. Either rating requires thermal and stability tests of defined duration. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC also need dedicated verification. The next step is to identify what limits sustained output, measure the effect of each controlled change, and use those findings to guide V3.
+Digitally controlled operation at approximately \SI{80}{\watt} was demonstrated. In the revised plan, a \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and a \SI{65}{\watt} continuous minimum are retained. Thermal and stability tests of defined duration are required for either rating. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC also need dedicated verification. The next step is to identify what limits sustained output, measure the effect of each controlled change, and use those findings to guide V3.
